 ## Networking notes
 
-- **IPv6 is link-local only, and only on the USB-C LAN (`usb0`).** It exists
+- **One LAN bridge.** `S30gadget_nic` creates the Linux bridge `br-lan` at
+  boot (kernel `CONFIG_BRIDGE=y`) with `usb0` as a port and `10.42.0.1/24` on
+  the bridge; while the hotspot runs, hostapd adds the AP interface (`wlan0`
+  in AP mode, `wlan1` in STA + AP) as a second port. STP is off with no
+  forwarding delay (the USB host's first DHCP request isn't lost), and
+  multicast snooping is off, so AutoInterface's multicast reaches every port.
+  The bridge keeps the box's per-unit USB MAC. The ports carry no address;
+  traffic between them is switched at L2 and not filtered, and nftables sees
+  every LAN client as `br-lan`. `br-lan` exists even with no USB host and no
+  radio (Nano-e). If the bridge can't be made, `usb0` is addressed directly
+  as before and the hotspot stays off (the WiFi tab says why). The LAN
+  interface in use is recorded in `/run/lan-iface` (`br-lan`, or `usb0`).
+- **IPv6 is link-local only, and only on the LAN (`br-lan`).** It exists
   solely for Reticulum's `AutoInterface`, which discovers peers over IPv6
   link-local multicast (`ff12::`, UDP 29716/29717, data on 42671). Every other
-  interface — `eth0`, the WiFi client and hotspot, `sl0` — has IPv6 disabled
-  (`S09ipv6`); the box does no IPv6 routing, NAT, RA or DHCPv6, has no IPv6
-  internet, and dnsmasq filters AAAA records for LAN clients.
-- For AutoInterface discovery, the host's USB network adapter must have IPv6
-  enabled (the default on Windows, macOS and Linux). Otherwise point a
-  `TCPClientInterface` at `10.42.0.1:4242`. Hotspot clients always use TCP
-  (`192.168.42.1:4242`); AutoInterface does not run on the hotspot.
-- RNS adopts an AutoInterface interface only when `rnsd` starts, and `usb0`
-  gets its link-local address only once a USB host brings the link up. If the
-  host attaches after `rnsd` started (box powered separately, or a slow first
-  driver install), `S46wanwatch` restarts `rnsd` once when the address appears.
-- **Firewall fail-safe.** The ruleset is regenerated at boot and
-  pre-validated (`nft -c`) before it is swapped in. If generation fails on a
-  first boot, the shipped fallback `/etc/nftables.conf` is LAN-only: the
-  USB-C LAN keeps SSH, the web UI and NAT'd internet, while no WAN SSH, port
